{{--
  Click handler for the #theme-toggle button.

  Included by partials/app-nav.stx, partials/marketing-footer.stx and
  index.stx, which each carried an identical copy before migration Phase 5.
  A page without a #theme-toggle button includes this harmlessly.

  ## What this used to do, and why none of it is here now

  It was a plain script block that did `document.getElementById('theme-toggle')`,
  bound a click listener to it, read `data-theme` off <html>, resolved that
  against `matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)')`, flipped it, wrote the
  attribute back and then `localStorage.setItem('uptimestatus-theme', next)`.
  Three of those are prohibited by stx-standards rule 8 -- getElementById,
  addEventListener and window.localStorage -- and all three have a named
  replacement that is auto-imported into a client script block.

  `useColorMode()` replaces the whole middle of it. It reads the app's own
  configuration out of `window.__STX_COLOR_MODE__`, which the pre-paint boot
  script publishes from config/ui.ts `app.colorMode` -- the same
  `uptimestatus-theme` key and the same `data-theme` attribute this file used
  to hardcode, so the stored preference carries over and the boot script keeps
  agreeing with it. `toggle()` is precisely what the old handler computed:
  flip the RESOLVED theme, which already accounts for the OS preference when
  nothing is stored.

  Verified on the served page before relying on it, because this app
  deliberately removed `app.colorMode` once before (0.2.401) when the serve
  path emitted a boot script that ignored `respectExisting`. config/ui.ts
  names the check -- curl a page and grep for `forced` -- and it passes: the
  boot script carries `forced`, and publishes storageKey, attribute and
  respectExisting.

  Two things come free that the old code did not have: `useColorMode`
  subscribes to the cross-tab `storage` event, so changing the theme in one
  tab now updates the others, and it registers an onDestroy cleanup.

  ## Why the listener is delegated rather than bound to the button

  The button lives in partials/app-nav.stx and partials/marketing-nav.stx, not
  here, so `useRef('name')` cannot reach it -- a ref resolves inside the scope
  that declares it. Delegating from document is the same choice
  partials/confirm-guard.stx makes, and for the same second reason: it keeps
  working across the router's fragment swaps.

  `useEventListener` rather than addEventListener is what makes that safe.
  This block is `data-stx-run="always"`, so it re-runs on every navigation; a
  raw document listener would accumulate one per page visit and the toggle
  would start flipping twice, then three times. useEventListener registers an
  onDestroy cleanup, so the previous one is torn down first. Measured, not
  assumed: the toggle still flips exactly once after five navigations.

  Exactly one copy of this runs per page -- the served scope ids are
  `stx_scope_theme_toggle_N_…` and there is one on / and one on /dashboard --
  so the delegated handler has no twin to race.
--}}
<script client>
  const theme = useColorMode()

  // Signature note, because the two copies in the installed package disagree
  // and the auto-import list does not give a shape: dist/browser-composables.js
  // takes (target, event, handler), but the runtime actually served --
  // /_stx/runtime.*.js -- takes (event, handler, { target }). Calling it the
  // other way throws "parameter 2 is not of type 'Object'" from
  // addEventListener and the toggle silently does nothing. Measured on the
  // served page; the served copy is the one that counts.
  useEventListener('click', (event) => {
    const target = event.target
    if (target && typeof target.closest === 'function' && target.closest('#theme-toggle'))
      theme.toggle()
  }, { target: document })
</script>
